prompt:
A real photograph, taken with a good phone camera and looking exactly like a phone photograph, of a patient being admitted to a hospital ward on a weekday morning, in a single patient room, taken by the nurse who is filling in the admission form, standing a few metres away by the door, in a tall vertical picture, everything in sharp focus from the floor in the foreground to the back wall. Natural colours, soft daylight from the window. The textures of real steel, plastic, cotton, leather, wool, paper and skin, with the true depth and the slight lens perspective of a photograph.

The reference photograph shows another room of the same hospital ward. This room is furnished the same way: the same pale-grey electric hospital bed with pale beech-wood boards, the same small white bedside cabinet with one drawer and an open shelf, the same warm white walls, the same pale grey vinyl floor and the same white window frame. Use the reference photograph for the room and the furniture only; the people here are new.

Every object stands on its own, with a little space around it, so that the eye can tell at once where each thing is. The whole room carries no writing, numbers, print, symbol or logo anywhere: every paper is blank or carries only grey lines and empty boxes, the cards are plain, the screen of the tablet shows only plain pale-blue panels, and the room looks as it could look in any modern hospital in Europe or America.

THE PATIENT, a woman of about seventy-four with short wavy silver-grey hair, round glasses with thin gold frames, a kind, slightly worried face and rosy cheeks, sits in a grey armchair with wooden arms in the middle of the picture, beside the made, empty bed. She wears a plum-coloured cardigan over a cream blouse, a grey wool skirt to below the knee and skin-coloured tights. Her foot on the left of the picture is wrapped in a white bandage and sits in a wide black bandage shoe with a velcro strap; her other foot is in a plain black walking shoe. Both feet stand flat on the floor, with their shadow under them. A plain white plastic band is around her wrist. She looks at her daughter with a small, trusting smile.

HER DAUGHTER, a woman of about forty-five with shoulder-length dark-blonde hair, in a navy-blue raincoat over a grey jumper and dark jeans, stands at the right of the armchair, turned towards her mother, a hand resting on the back of the armchair; with the other hand she holds out to her mother a blue cardboard document folder, closed.

THE OVERBED TABLE stands in front of the armchair, on the left of the picture: a white hospital table on a grey steel foot with castors, its top at the height of the armrests. On its top, each thing apart from the others, with a band of plain white tabletop around it:
- a brown clipboard holding a blank form ruled with grey lines and empty boxes, with a blue ballpoint pen lying across it;
- a plain green plastic card the size of a bank card;
- a plain pink sheet of paper lying flat;
- a plain white sheet ruled into an empty table with grey lines;
- a small plain yellow folded booklet the size of a passport;
- a small clear plastic zip bag holding a brown leather wallet, a wristwatch and a bunch of keys;
- a tablet computer in a grey cover, lying flat, its screen showing plain pale-blue panels;
- a grey blood pressure monitor with its folded grey arm cuff.

THE LEFT OF THE PICTURE: the white bedside cabinet beside the head of the bed, with the empty white bed behind the table; a wooden walking stick with a curved handle leans against the side of the armchair, on the left of the picture.

THE RIGHT OF THE PICTURE, on the floor: a dark-red hard suitcase on four wheels standing upright with its handle pulled up, and beside it a brown leather handbag. Against the right-hand wall, a flat grey floor scale with a white measuring rod for height fixed to the wall above it, a slim white upright rod with a sliding grey head piece; a tall white wardrobe with two closed doors stands beside it.

THE BACK WALL: a tall window with a white frame, closed, showing a pale sky and green trees; a round wall clock with a plain white face and twelve short black tick marks above the bed.

The floor is pale grey vinyl and the walls are warm white. The bottom of the picture shows the floor in front of the armchair and the table. The moment is calm and friendly, caught in the middle of the admission.
